  "verdict": {
    "kind": "move-after",
    "label": "Ready after 2 changes",
    "title": "You can move go-fly to Cloud Run after 2 changes",
    "reason": "The SQLite file and the timers need a change each before Cloud Run; the rest moves as it is."
  },
  "findings": [
    {
      "i": 1,
      "id": "sqlite-database",
      "kind": "breaks",
      "title": "SQLite on a volume as the database",
      "at": "main.go:15",
      "code": "db, err := sql.Open(\"sqlite\", os.Getenv(\"DB_PATH\"))",
      "why": "Cloud Run has no disk a database file can live on: the filesystem is memory, and each instance would hold its own copy that vanishes when it stops.",
      "fix": "Move the data to Firestore before the move: each table becomes a collection and each row a document, the database/sql calls become reads and writes through cloud.google.com/go/firestore, and a join becomes a second read or a field copied into the document.",
      "part": "sqlite",
      "kb": "cloud-sql-firestore-or-serverless-postgres",
      "subject": "the SQLite file",
      "task": 2
    },
    {
      "i": 2,
      "id": "in-process-timer",
      "kind": "breaks",
      "title": "Timers inside the process",
      "at": "main.go:36",
      "code": "ticker := time.NewTicker(10 * time.Minute)",
      "why": "A service at zero has no process to run a timer, a running one gets no CPU between requests, and with several instances the timer fires once per instance: late, never, or twice.",
      "fix": "Cloud Scheduler on the same cron lines: short work calls POST /cron/<name> on the service with an OIDC token, long work starts a Cloud Run job. Set each job's time zone; the timer ran on the server's, usually UTC.",
      "part": "web",
      "kb": "cron-and-timers-with-cloud-scheduler",
      "subject": "the timers",
      "task": 3
    }
  ],
  "clean": [
    "workers waiting on a queue",
